WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.400
-Welcome back to "late night."

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.400
We're here with the fantastic
Ana Gasteyer.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.934
Season 2 of any show
is exciting,

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:06.167
but this is such a good part.

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:07.434
Such a good clip earlier.

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:09.000
You play a bumbling CEO.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.033
I feel as though we see those
in the news more and more.

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:13.400
It seems like a "delightly" --

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.367
delightfully, excuse me,
fun character to play.

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:18.701
-It is a delightly
fun character to play.

00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:19.734
-Delightly.

00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:21.567
I think the way I said it
is right.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:22.734
-Delightly.
-Yeah.

00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:24.901
-It's delightly fun.

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:27.601
It is. It's amazing.
It's an ensemble cast.

00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:29.067
It's a half-hour, you know,

00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:33.200
workplace network comedy
in the great NBC tradition.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.601
I mean, it's single-camera.

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.667
Justin Spitzer,
another Northwestern grad.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:37.968
-Another Northwestern.

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:39.367
We're really shouting it out
a fair amount tonight,

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:42.067
but, you know, that's what
happens with excellence, yeah.

00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:43.601
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:47.234
And, believe me,
they need the help.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:50.601
-Hey, you have a very exciting
cameo coming up on Season 2.

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:52.734
-A little somebody to my left

00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:56.434
might be appearing on the show
for a couple of episodes.

00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:58.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:58.334 --> 00:01:00.801
-This is very fun because --
-It's so fun.

00:01:00.801 --> 00:01:03.434
-I don't think I have a ton
of range as an actor,

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:05.267
so I was very flattered
when you reached out

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:08.200
and asked if I wanted
to play myself.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.501
-No, it's pretty great
because we have so much --

00:01:11.501 --> 00:01:14.667
So I play this, you know,

00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:17.701
slightly in-over-her-head CEO,
as you mentioned.

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:21.534
First female CEO, big automotive
company, Payne Motors,

00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:23.434
grand old venerable American,

00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:26.734
you know,
manufacturing organization.

00:01:26.734 --> 00:01:29.367
And I come in,
and I'm an experienced

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:32.467
and seasoned CEO
from Big Pharma,

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:35.434
but I don't know anything
about cars, and I don't drive.

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:37.067
-Yeah.

00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:39.434
-And I'm of that school --
The character's of that school

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:41.601
of just like, "You don't need
to know the product.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:43.634
You just got to know how to
sell it and manage people."

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:45.133
So she's very confident,

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:47.801
and it's just one calamity
after another.

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:51.033
She really -- It's a show of a
bunch of people on the C-suite

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:54.767
desperately trying to cover
their own asses, just like life.

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:57.968
-Yeah.
-And so she does -- she gets --

00:01:57.968 --> 00:02:01.801
We ended last season with
kind of a huge PR crisis.

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:04.968
And her skill set was this kind
of super calm under pressure

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:06.834
due to all
the pharma experience.

00:02:06.834 --> 00:02:09.834
And we came back at
the beginning of this season

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:12.334
with a biblical-level crisis,

00:02:12.334 --> 00:02:15.367
and I become the most hated CEO
in the country,

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:17.200
and you get to make fun of me
on your show.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.634
-And then you come as a guest
on my show.

00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:20.000
-I do.
-And I'm very excited to see it.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.634
And that's I think
in a couple of weeks.

00:02:21.634 --> 00:02:22.767
-Yes, it is.
I think it's Episode 2.

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:24.667
-We have worked together
over the years,

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:27.133
but we only overlapped
one year together on "SNL."

00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:28.968
And then you threw me
many bones,

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:30.434
because this was
a time in my life

00:02:30.434 --> 00:02:32.033
where I was just happy
to be in anything, right?

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:34.200
Season one on "SNL,"
you will take any part.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.200
You played a character
named Sarah Blazen

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.834
and were very kind
to let me be a backup dancer,

00:02:40.834 --> 00:02:42.367
which, by the way,
was probably the only thing

00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:44.701
I did in the show that week,
so that is --

00:02:44.701 --> 00:02:46.901
-No, but I have
such a fond memory.

00:02:46.901 --> 00:02:48.834
Okay, so this is one
of those sketches.

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:50.434
I did it twice.
This was my --

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:52.801
This, I think,
was the first time.

00:02:52.801 --> 00:02:54.467
Another James Anderson
spectacular.

00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:56.901
-Yeah.
-She was an old Broadway dame.

00:02:56.901 --> 00:02:59.167
She would come in if
Carol Channing was hit by a bus

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:00.901
or something like that.
-Yeah.

00:03:00.901 --> 00:03:02.334
-You know, Leslie Uggams,

00:03:02.334 --> 00:03:04.467
something terrible
would befall her,

00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:06.434
and they would bring in Blazen
at the last --

00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:07.934
But she had a lot of issues.

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:09.534
She had a pin in her hip,

00:03:09.534 --> 00:03:11.934
so she would not do any
of the choreography.

00:03:11.934 --> 00:03:12.901
Do you remember that?

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:14.067
And she would keep
referring to it.

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:15.234
They would say, like,
"Hey, what if you get up

00:03:15.234 --> 00:03:16.400
and you do a jazz square?"

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.100
♪ I got a pin in my hip ♪

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.501
Yeah, so, um, she was --
she was -- she was very sassy.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.234
Thank you.
She was very sassy.

00:03:23.234 --> 00:03:25.267
And I'll never forget you --

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:28.734
I have such a clear, like,
feeling of joy

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:31.334
that you loved the character
at the rewrite table,

00:03:31.334 --> 00:03:34.067
because I remember
she would bitch and moan

00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:35.567
and make all these people,
like, serve her

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:36.901
and bring the key
down really low

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:39.133
'cause she'd smoked and drank
her whole life.

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:41.868
And then at, like, the 11th
hour, she would just quit.

00:03:41.868 --> 00:03:44.000
-Yeah, right before it started,
she would quit.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.067
She would make you
meet everything.

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:47.767
-And I remember
that you loved that.

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:50.834
-And here is --
This was another "SNL" host

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:53.767
that I think not a lot of people
remember, Jonny Moseley.

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:55.167
-Yeah, skier Jonny Moseley.

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.400
-Skier Jonny Moseley.
Like, trick skier.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.534
I feel like he had
a move called...

00:03:59.534 --> 00:04:01.234
-What was it?
-...Jelly Roll, maybe?

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:02.467
-It involved some--

00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:04.334
-It was spinning in the air
on skis.

00:04:04.334 --> 00:04:06.901
We're not making this up.
-The Dinner Roll. Google it.

00:04:06.901 --> 00:04:08.767
-I know we're
very good improvisers.

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:10.434
You might be like,
"Oh, they're doing a bit."

00:04:10.434 --> 00:04:12.601
-It was called the Dinner Roll.
I don't know.

00:04:12.601 --> 00:04:15.367
-I should note, I do want
to stress, lovely guy.

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:16.701
I enjoyed the week a great deal.
-So nice.

00:04:16.701 --> 00:04:17.934
-This is a very unfair photo,

00:04:17.934 --> 00:04:20.968
'cause it seems like
I'm jealous right now.

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:23.000
-And also, I mean,
he put on a dance tight.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.267
-Yeah, he put on a dance tight.
-You go, Jonny Moseley.

00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:27.067
-You medaled for me.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:29.167
I'm very excited about this.

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:31.634
You did a wonderful parody

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:34.334
of the sort of
Hallmark Christmas movies

00:04:34.334 --> 00:04:35.968
or Christmas movies in general.

00:04:35.968 --> 00:04:37.334
-No, Hallmark,
very specifically.

00:04:37.334 --> 00:04:38.868
-I feel like last time
when I said Hallmark,

00:04:38.868 --> 00:04:40.133
you guys said, "Whaa."

00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:41.501
-It was probably
just 'cause Viacom

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:44.968
didn't want us to say that.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:04:44.968 --> 00:04:46.567
So now the truth is out.

00:04:46.567 --> 00:04:48.801
And it was you
and Rachel Dratch.

00:04:48.801 --> 00:04:51.033
And it was
"A Clusterfunke Christmas."

00:04:51.033 --> 00:04:54.200
And these are -- I mean, there's
like an industrial complex

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.567
of Christmas movies.
-This is right.

00:04:55.567 --> 00:04:58.467
-And they have some
really wonderful tropes

00:04:58.467 --> 00:05:00.334
that recur often.
-They're all the same movie.

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:02.834
They're all a very uptight,
bitchy executive

00:05:02.834 --> 00:05:05.634
goes usually to a small town
at Christmas,

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:07.801
and she wants to buy the inn
and destroy it

00:05:07.801 --> 00:05:09.734
and turn it into a parking
complex and, I don't know,

00:05:09.734 --> 00:05:12.367
indoor water ski facility.
-Yeah.

00:05:12.367 --> 00:05:15.400
-And she doesn't
believe in Christmas.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.133
Of course she hates Christmas,
and she hates love.

00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:21.667
So she comes to the small town
and then miraculously meets

00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:28.334
a non-January-6th-participating
lumberjack, and...

00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:29.634
it's amazing.

00:05:29.634 --> 00:05:33.367
And she -- And she --

00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:35.734
He takes her into
his be-flanneled arms.

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:37.167
And she --

00:05:37.167 --> 00:05:38.801
And also the other thing
I love about these movies,

00:05:38.801 --> 00:05:41.634
they have one date, and then
she gives up her entire career.

00:05:41.634 --> 00:05:43.601
Usually, like, you know,
Harvard law.

00:05:43.601 --> 00:05:45.567
She's like, "Screw it.
I'm staying at the inn."

00:05:45.567 --> 00:05:49.567
-Yeah, and it's like a for sure
all-the-clothes-stay-on romance.

00:05:49.567 --> 00:05:50.868
Like, you know what I mean?

00:05:50.868 --> 00:05:53.701
One date where it's like
they maybe kiss on the cheek

00:05:53.701 --> 00:05:55.734
after a cocoa.
-So there really are --

00:05:55.734 --> 00:05:58.234
We produced it with our friend
Danielle von Zerneck,

00:05:58.234 --> 00:06:00.667
who has produced a lot
of actual Hallmark movies.

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:02.434
And so we wanted -- as I'm sure

00:06:02.434 --> 00:06:03.901
in your "Documentary Now!"
division --

00:06:03.901 --> 00:06:05.534
-Right, you want accuracy.

00:06:05.534 --> 00:06:06.801
-Absolutely picture --

00:06:06.801 --> 00:06:08.400
There's Christmas
in every frame.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.267
There's all these rules.
Like there's a --

00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:15.033
There was a plaid ban
for a long time at Hallmark.

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:16.868
So when we went
to start costuming,

00:06:16.868 --> 00:06:19.033
the costumer,
completely seriously, said,

00:06:19.033 --> 00:06:21.400
"Now, are we honoring
the plaid ban?"

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.167
-Now, what brought --
-I don't know.

00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:26.000
Some exec had a real beef
with plaid.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.167
I don't know if something just
was a trigger area for that --

00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:33.834
Yeah, so Dratch and I play
these old innkeepers who,

00:06:33.834 --> 00:06:36.133
again, look at the picture,
are in their 40s.

00:06:36.133 --> 00:06:39.234
But in these movies --
in these movies,

00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:42.267
you're either a breeder
or an old prune.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:46.667
So we were not the breeder.

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:48.534
The lovely Vella Lovell was.

00:06:48.534 --> 00:06:50.367
But we --
So we're writing a sequel.

00:06:50.367 --> 00:06:51.667
-You're writing a sequel,

00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:55.000
and this one is now visiting
one of the new tropes.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.501
-We are -- The area this time
is a royal holiday.

00:06:59.501 --> 00:07:01.868
-A royal holiday, because there
is -- I will say, a lot of them

00:07:01.868 --> 00:07:03.367
seem to have "prince"
or "princess" in the title.

00:07:03.367 --> 00:07:05.100
-Yes, there's another
whole trope

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.434
where you either didn't know
you were royalty

00:07:07.434 --> 00:07:09.300
or you secretly become a nanny

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:10.868
and the prince
falls in love with you.

00:07:10.868 --> 00:07:12.167
-Yeah.
-There's a lot of --

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:15.133
So the premise at the moment
is that we go

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:19.767
to an international
strudel competition in --

00:07:19.767 --> 00:07:21.567
'cause we're
world-renowned strudelers.

00:07:21.567 --> 00:07:23.767
-Yeah.
-And the -- And two princes,

00:07:23.767 --> 00:07:26.167
for reasons not clear to us,
fall in love with us.

00:07:26.167 --> 00:07:27.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.601 --> 00:07:29.400
-Well, I cannot wait for that.
-Thank you.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.000
-And I can't wait for
"American Auto" to come back.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.801
You guys, that's Ana Gasteyer.
-Thank you.

00:07:33.801 --> 00:07:35.133
-Her show "American Auto" --

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:36.567
Tuesday nights, 8:30,
here on NBC.

00:07:36.567 --> 00:07:38.467
We'll be right back
with Bomani Jones.

